### How do you register a shutdown hook?

A shutdown hook is a Thread that you register with the JVM. The JVM starts it during orderly shutdown. The thread must be created but not started by you; the JVM starts it.

```java
package com.bank.shutdown;

public class ShutdownHookRegistration {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> {
            System.out.println("Shutdown hook: flushing audit buffer...");
            auditBuffer.flush();
            connectionPool.close();
            System.out.println("Shutdown hook: cleanup complete.");
        }, "cleanup-hook"));
    }
}
```

### When does it run and when does it not?

| Trigger | Hook Runs? | Notes |
|---------|------------|-------|
| Last non-daemon thread finishes | Yes | JVM exits naturally |
| System.exit(code) | Yes | Explicit shutdown |
| SIGTERM (Kubernetes pod stop) | Yes | k8s sends SIGTERM first |
| Ctrl-C (SIGINT) | Yes | Developer stop |
| kill -9 (SIGKILL) | No | Immediate process death |
| Power loss | No | Hardware failure |
| Native JVM crash (segfault) | No | Unrecoverable error |
| OutOfMemoryError | Maybe | Depends on how JVM handles it |

### What is the graceful shutdown pattern?

The standard pattern stops accepting new work, drains in-flight tasks with a bounded timeout, and then closes resources.

```java
package com.bank.shutdown;

import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public class GracefulShutdown {

    private volatile boolean running = true;
    private final ExecutorService executor;

    public GracefulShutdown(ExecutorService executor) {
        this.executor = executor;
        Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(this::shutdown, "graceful-shutdown"));
    }

    private void shutdown() {
        running = false;              // signal to stop accepting work
        executor.shutdown();          // stop accepting new tasks
        try {
            if (!executor.awaitTermination(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
                var dropped = executor.shutdownNow();  // interrupt stragglers
                System.err.println("Dropped " + dropped.size() + " tasks");
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            executor.shutdownNow();
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
        System.out.println("Shutdown complete.");
    }

    public boolean isRunning() { return running; }
}
```

### How does Kubernetes interact with shutdown hooks?

Kubernetes sends SIGTERM to the pod's main process. The JVM receives SIGTERM and runs registered shutdown hooks. Kubernetes waits for `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` (default 30 seconds) before sending SIGKILL. Your shutdown hook must complete within this grace period.

```text
Kubernetes:  SIGTERM ──── 30s grace ──── SIGKILL
JVM:         hooks start ──── drain tasks ──── exit
```

### How do multiple shutdown hooks interact?

All registered hooks start concurrently in separate threads. They run in no guaranteed order. If you need ordering, use a single hook that performs steps sequentially.

```java
// WRONG: relying on hook ordering (undefined)
Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> closeConnections()));
Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> flushBuffers()));
// Connections might close BEFORE buffers flush!

// CORRECT: single hook with ordered steps
Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> {
    flushBuffers();      // step 1
    closeConnections();  // step 2
}));
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: calling System.exit inside a hook (deadlocks)
Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> {
    cleanup();
    System.exit(0);  // DEADLOCK: shutdown is already in progress
}));

// WRONG: relying on hook for critical data
Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> {
    saveUncommittedPayments();  // may not run on kill -9!
}));

// CORRECT: commit transactionally as you go
@Transactional
void processPayment(Payment payment) {
    paymentRepo.save(payment.withStatus("COMPLETED"));
    // Committed immediately, survives any shutdown scenario
}
```

### How does Spring Boot handle graceful shutdown?

Spring Boot 2.3+ supports graceful shutdown with `server.shutdown=graceful` and `spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase=30s`. When SIGTERM is received, Spring stops accepting new requests, waits for in-flight requests to complete, and then destroys beans in reverse order.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do multiple shutdown hooks interact? They start concurrently, no order guaranteed.
- What is the relationship between daemon threads and shutdown hooks? When all non-daemon threads finish, shutdown hooks run, and then daemon threads are killed.
- Can you remove a registered shutdown hook? Yes, with `Runtime.removeShutdownHook(thread)`, but only before shutdown has started.
